## 6. DESIGN SYSTEM RULES

These are not aesthetic preferences. They are product decisions tied to the wellness philosophy.

### Color palette (Tailwind custom config)
```js
colors: {
  cream: {
    50: '#FDFBF7',
    100: '#F9F5EE',
    200: '#F0E9DA',
  },
  sage: {
    100: '#E8EFE4',
    200: '#C9DBC2',
    400: '#87AA7E',
    600: '#4E7A44',
    800: '#2C4827',
  },
  slate: {
    warm: '#4A4A45',
    muted: '#7A7A74',
    hint: '#ADADAA',
  },
  sky: {
    soft: '#EAF2F8',
    mid: '#A8C8E0',
    deep: '#4A8AB5',
  },
  amber: {
    warm: '#F2A65A',
    glow: '#E8845A',
  }
}
```

### Typography
- Body text: minimum 16px, line-height 1.7
- Never use pure black (#000) or pure white (#FFF) — use cream-50 and slate-warm
- Headings: font-weight 500 only, never 700 (too aggressive)
- Font family: `'Instrument Serif'` for display/headings, `'DM Sans'` for UI/body

### Spacing
- Generous padding: minimum p-6 on cards, p-8 on screens
- Content breathes — never pack elements tightly

### What is NEVER allowed in the UI
- Red notification badges or dot counts
- Countdown timers or urgency indicators
- "X people are watching this" or similar social proof pressure
- Follower/following counts shown publicly on feeds or profile cards (only on own profile in settings)
- Like/resonance counts shown on posts in the feed (only visible to the post author)
- "Trending" labels or sections in the main feed
- Autoplay video with sound
- Pop-up modals that interrupt browsing (full-screen gentle overlays are fine)
- Push notifications for: like counts, follower milestones, "you haven't posted in X days"

---

## 7. THE WELLNESS ENGINE — CORE BEHAVIOURAL RULES

The WellnessContext wraps the entire app. It must be implemented before any feed or content features.

### Session time thresholds
- 20 minutes: Show soft top banner — dismiss with one tap, no guilt
- 40 minutes: Blur feed, show full-screen rest screen (cannot be immediately dismissed — 5 second minimum)
- 60 minutes: Lock feed for 10 minutes, show breathing exercise

### Rest screen requirements
- Full screen, not a modal
- Background: rotating Unsplash nature image (curated: forest, ocean, mountain, sky — never cities or crowds)
- AI-generated affirming message based on user's recent mood
- Animated breathing circle (4s inhale, 4s hold, 4s exhale)
- Two options: "I'm done for now" (primary, large) and "Keep browsing" (secondary, small, appears after 5 seconds)
- Log user choice to wellness_events

### Daily feed cap
- 30 post impressions per day maximum (main feed)
- After cap: show end screen, not an error
- End screen message: warm, affirming, never guilt-inducing
- Discovery tab: 10 posts, refreshes once at midnight only

---

## 8. FEED ALGORITHM — INVIOLABLE RULES

The feed algorithm lives in `/supabase/functions/feed/index.ts` and `/lib/feed/`.

### Scoring weights
- Relationship depth: 40%
- Mood-time fit: 30%
- Recency (linear decay over 48h): 20%
- Serendipity (stable random, seeded by user_id + date): 10%

### Hard constraints — never override these
- Never ORDER BY or weight by: likes count, comments count, shares, follower count, watch time, viral velocity
- Never surface a "trending" feed or sort
- Maximum 2 consecutive posts from the same creator
- Posts older than 48 hours are excluded from main feed entirely
- Return maximum 15 posts per page request
- Enforce 30-post daily cap server-side (not just client-side)

### Interaction graph scoring
```
score += 4.0  for each DM exchanged
score += 3.0  for each reply to their post
score += 2.5  for each post saved
score += 1.5  for each comment left
score -= 0.5  for each "scrolled past" event (tracked server-side)
```
Cache interaction graph in personality_profiles.interaction_graph, update async.

---

## 9. AI LAYER — ANTHROPIC INTEGRATION

### Model
Always use: `claude-sonnet-4-20250514`
Always set: `max_tokens: 1024` for companion messages, `max_tokens: 256` for post analysis

### Post companion system prompt
```
You are the Serene companion — a warm, grounding presence for this user.
When responding to their post, be specific about the actual content: the light, the moment, the detail they chose to share.
Do not say: "Great photo!", "Love this!", "Beautiful!", or any generic affirmation.
Do say: something specific about what you actually see or feel in the content.
Ask one gentle, open-ended question if appropriate. Keep your response under 3 sentences.
Never suggest the user needs more followers, more engagement, or to post more often.
Respond in the same language the user wrote their caption in.
```

### Wellness chat system prompt
```
You are the Serene companion. You are warm, grounding, and calm.
You are not a therapist and do not provide clinical advice.
When users share struggles, validate their feeling briefly, then gently redirect toward something grounding (breath, the present moment, something small and good).
If a user expresses crisis-level distress or mentions self-harm, always provide crisis resources immediately and encourage them to reach out to a professional.
Keep responses short (2-4 sentences) unless the user is clearly seeking a longer conversation.
Never ask multiple questions at once.
```

### What the AI must never do
- Generate comparative statements ("other people feel this way too" framing that implies competition)
- Suggest users post more, engage more, or grow their audience
- Reference metrics, follower counts, or popularity
- Provide clinical diagnosis or therapy
- Generate content that could be mistaken for crisis counselling

---

## 10. ETHICAL ADS — HARD CONSTRAINTS

### Allowed ad categories (whitelist — nothing outside this list)
```
mental_health_apps
physical_wellness          ← yoga, fitness, physiotherapy
healthy_food_cooking       ← ingredients, cookware, meal prep
nature_outdoor_activities  ← hiking, gardening, camping
creativity_tools           ← art supplies, music, writing tools
education                  ← courses, books, learning platforms
sustainable_products       ← eco-friendly goods with verified claims
sleep_aids                 ← non-pharmaceutical
local_community_events
```

### Blocked ad categories (blacklist — enforce at insertion, not just display)
```
fast_fashion
gambling
alcohol
tobacco
weight_loss (unless prescribed medical programme with verification)
beauty_filters_or_surgery
social_comparison_products (anything marketed with "get more followers", "go viral", etc.)
fomo_promotions (flash sales, countdown timers, "only X left")
cryptocurrency
financial_trading
```

### Ad placement rules
- Maximum 1 ad per 10 posts in feed
- Ad must be clearly labeled "Partner" — never disguised as organic content
- No ad retargeting based on behaviour outside Serene
- No third-party ad pixels or tracking scripts
- Targeting uses only: personality_type, mood_tag preferences, time_of_day (never demographic data)
- Users can dismiss any ad category permanently

---

## 11. SECURITY & PRIVACY RULES

- All personality_profile data: RLS policy allows only the owning user to read/write
- Never expose personality_profile data via any public API endpoint
- Never log post content or captions to any analytics service
- Interaction graph: stored server-side only, never sent to client
- Ad targeting: computed server-side, the client never receives the targeting reason (only the ad itself)
- Session duration: tracked in wellness_events, never sent to third parties
- Media uploads: validate file type server-side (not just client-side), scan for CSAM via PhotoDNA or equivalent before storage
- Auth: use Supabase Auth with email + OAuth (Google, Apple). Implement PKCE flow for mobile.
- Rate limiting: implement on all AI endpoints (max 20 companion messages per user per hour)

---

## 12. WHAT TO DO BEFORE WRITING ANY CODE

When starting a new feature or session:

1. Re-read sections 6, 7, 8, and 9 of this file
2. Confirm the feature does not violate any rule in sections 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, or 11
3. Identify which database tables the feature touches
4. Identify which RLS policies need to exist or be updated
5. Identify whether the feature needs an AI call — if so, confirm which system prompt applies
6. Propose the implementation plan in plain English before writing code
7. Write types first, then server logic, then UI components

---

## 13. FEATURE PRIORITY ORDER (build in this sequence)

### Phase 1 — Foundation
1. Database schema + RLS policies (all tables)
2. Supabase Auth (email + Google OAuth)
3. WellnessContext (session tracking, time thresholds)
4. Basic user profile (create, edit, view)

### Phase 2 — Core Social
5. Post creation (photo, text, mood tag)
6. Feed algorithm (Edge Function)
7. Follow system
8. Resonance system (no public counts)
9. Comments

### Phase 3 — AI Layer
10. Post companion (AI message on upload)
11. Personality profile builder (passive, from posts)
12. Wellness chat companion

### Phase 4 — Wellness Features
13. Rest screen + breathing exercise
14. Daily cap + end screen
15. Discovery tab

### Phase 5 — Creator Tools
16. Stories (24hr)
17. Video posts
18. Creator analytics dashboard
19. Post scheduler

### Phase 6 — Monetisation
20. Ethical ads system
21. Creator subscriptions (Stripe)
22. Tip jar

---

## 14. HOW TO HANDLE AMBIGUITY

If a feature request or user story is ambiguous, default to the more conservative, wellness-preserving interpretation. Examples:

- "Show post engagement" → show only to the post author, never publicly
- "Notify the user" → ask whether this serves the user or serves engagement before implementing
- "Add a trending section" → do not implement. Raise this with the team first.
- "Increase session time" → this is never a goal. Reframe the metric.
- "Recommend content" → use personality-based curation only, never viral-signal-based

When genuinely unsure, add a `// WELLNESS-CHECK:` comment explaining the decision made and why, so it can be reviewed.

---

## 15. DEFINITION OF DONE

A feature is done when:
- [ ] TypeScript types are defined in `/types/`
- [ ] RLS policies exist for all database operations
- [ ] No `any` types in production code
- [ ] Mobile and web implementations are in sync
- [ ] The WellnessContext session timer is not disrupted
- [ ] No engagement metrics are exposed publicly
- [ ] Error states are handled gracefully (no raw error messages shown to users)
- [ ] Loading states use skeleton screens, not spinners
- [ ] The feature has been checked against sections 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, and 11 of this file

